WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.727
Welcome to Module Nine: Safety, Ethics, and Compliance.

2
00:00:04.057 --> 00:00:07.506
Agents read untrusted content, then take real actions.

3
00:00:08.306 --> 00:00:12.629
Picture a small firm whose AI assistant runs its inbox and calendar.

4
00:00:12.959 --> 00:00:15.725
It drafts replies, books meetings, and sends files.

5
00:00:16.525 --> 00:00:19.099
One day, an ordinary-looking email arrives.

6
00:00:19.429 --> 00:00:22.771
Hidden inside is text written for the assistant, not a human.

7
00:00:23.571 --> 00:00:25.911
The assistant follows that text like an order.

8
00:00:26.241 --> 00:00:29.945
It quietly forwards confidential contracts to an outside address.

9
00:00:30.745 --> 00:00:32.743
Nobody on the team did anything wrong.

10
00:00:33.073 --> 00:00:37.845
This is indirect prompt injection: the attack hides in content the agent reads.

11
00:00:38.645 --> 00:00:41.581
The only defence was one line in the system prompt.

12
00:00:41.911 --> 00:00:45.915
But prompts are defaults attackers can talk over, not a security boundary.

13
00:00:46.715 --> 00:00:49.523
When the client asks what happened, nobody can answer.

14
00:00:49.853 --> 00:00:52.577
There's no audit trail to reconstruct the decision.

15
00:00:53.377 --> 00:00:56.932
The rebuild uses defence in depth, since any control can fail.

16
00:00:57.262 --> 00:00:59.899
Email is treated as data, not instructions,

17
00:00:59.949 --> 00:01:02.524
and rails screen it for injected commands.

18
00:01:03.324 --> 00:01:06.597
The assistant gets least privilege: only the tools it needs,

19
00:01:06.647 --> 00:01:08.757
checked against the user's permissions.

20
00:01:09.087 --> 00:01:12.279
Sending files outside now needs a person's approval.

21
00:01:13.079 --> 00:01:16.272
Outbound traffic can only reach approved destinations.

22
00:01:16.602 --> 00:01:18.663
And an audit trail records who asked,

23
00:01:18.713 --> 00:01:21.459
what was read, which tools ran, and who approved.

24
00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:25.793
Lesson one covers guardrails, runtime checks around the model.

25
00:01:26.123 --> 00:01:31.662
NeMo Guardrails defines five: input, dialog, retrieval, execution, and output.

26
00:01:32.462 --> 00:01:35.975
Exam questions give one requirement and ask which rail fits.

27
00:01:36.305 --> 00:01:39.540
Restricting which tools may run? That's the execution rail.

28
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.539
Lesson two covers prompt injection,

29
00:01:42.589 --> 00:01:45.879
which has no complete fix, only layered containment.

30
00:01:46.209 --> 00:01:49.999
Plus excessive agency, sandboxed code, and memory poisoning.

31
00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:52.598
Lesson three covers privacy:

32
00:01:52.648 --> 00:01:56.360
send only the data you need, and redact personal details.

33
00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:00.373
Deletions must reach every copy, not just the main database.

34
00:02:01.173 --> 00:02:05.198
A real audit trail is tamper-evident and can reconstruct any decision.

35
00:02:05.528 --> 00:02:10.171
You'll also check fairness by subgroup and meet frameworks like the EU AI Act.

36
00:02:10.971 --> 00:02:14.864
This domain is five percent of the exam, and it rewards one habit:

37
00:02:14.914 --> 00:02:17.492
put controls in the runtime, not the prompt.

38
00:02:17.822 --> 00:02:19.884
Lesson one starts with the five rails.
